<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonardo III Tocco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1503">1503</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tocco-family">Tocco family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonardo III Tocco was the last Tocco ruler of the County Palatine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> and titular despot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, from 1448. He lost his remaining mainland territories to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and in 1479 Ottoman forces took Cephalonia, Zakynthos and Lefkada, forcing him to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> later took the islands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonardo-iii-tocco.jpg" alt="Leonardo III Tocco" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlo Sellitto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonardo-di-tocco-duke-of-zakynthos-carlo-sellitto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leonardo was the eldest son of Carlo II Tocco and Raimondina Ventimiglia, daughter of the baron Giovanni Ventimiglia. His family were Italian nobles: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco">Carlo I Tocco</a>, his father's uncle, had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> in 1411, been recognised as despot by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-ii-palaiologos">Manuel II Palaiologos</a> in 1415, and captured Arta in 1416. Leonardo was still under age when his father died on 30 September 1448, and a council of four regents governed for him. Arta fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> on 24 March 1449, and of the mainland only Vonitza, Varnazza and Angelokastro remained to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Once of age, Leonardo had his four regents put to death. He secured Venetian protection and became an honorary citizen of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, though its help was often only nominal. In 1460 he joined the Albanian lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> in throwing off Ottoman suzerainty and lost two of his three mainland fortresses, keeping only Vonitza. During the First Ottoman–Venetian War his islands took in thousands of Christian refugees and flourished in peace. He revived the Orthodox Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> in 1452, employed more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and issued charters in Greek, but many Orthodox subjects still saw him as a tyrant, and strong earthquakes struck the islands in 1469.</p><p>After the death of his first wife, Milica Branković, in 1464, he married Francesca Marzano, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferdinand I of Naples</a>, in 1477. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, opposed to Neapolitan influence, left him out of its 1479 peace with Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, and his own peace bound him to an annual tribute of 4,000 ducats. When he offered a visiting Ottoman official fruit instead of the agreed 500 ducats, the official complained, and Mehmed sent 29 ships under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedik-ahmed-pasha">Gedik Ahmed Pasha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leonardo fled from Lefkada to the fortress of St. George on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> and then, with his wife, his son Carlo and his brothers Giovanni and Antonio, on a Venetian ship to Taranto. Ferdinand gave him a pension and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> of Briatico and Calimera in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> received him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1480. His brother Antonio briefly retook Cephalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> in 1481 but was killed by his own garrison in 1483. Leonardo served Ferdinand as a diplomat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, bought Sinopoli for 20,000 ducats and fell into debt. He died in Rome under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> when his house collapsed on him, and his son Carlo III Tocco inherited his claims.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leonardo III Tocco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Leonardo III Tocco</b> lost his mainland lands to the Ottomans and in 1479 the islands as well, fleeing to Italy.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-cephalonia-and-zakynthos">County Palatine of Cephalonia and Zakynthos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1493" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Leonardo III Tocco"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1493">What was here in 1493? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
